body:[
{paragraphs:['Makara Sankranti arrives differently from most Hindu festivals because its date is tied to the solar calendar. In 2026 it falls on 14 January. The day marks the Sun’s entry into Makara and is associated with Uttarayana, the northward movement of the Sun in traditional reckoning.','The same seasonal turning is celebrated under many names. Tamil Nadu has Pongal. Gujarat fills the sky with kites during Uttarayan. Karnataka families exchange ellu-bella. Maharashtra has til-gul. Punjab’s Lohri stands nearby in the winter harvest season. The names and rituals change, but food, Sun, land and gratitude keep returning.']},
{heading:'Why is Makara Sankranti a solar festival?',paragraphs:['Many Hindu festivals move through the Gregorian calendar because they follow lunar tithis. Makara Sankranti is tied to the Sun’s movement into Makara, so it remains near the middle of January.','That solar basis gives the festival a different rhythm. It is less about one mythic event and more about a seasonal crossing: longer days, harvest, warmth and the promise of another agricultural cycle.']},
{heading:'What is Pongal?',paragraphs:['Pongal is the great Tamil harvest festival. The word pongal means to boil or overflow, and the name is also used for the rice dish cooked with fresh harvest. When the pot boils over, abundance becomes visible.','The festival cycle can include Bhogi, Surya Pongal, Mattu Pongal and Kaanum Pongal. Each day shifts attention: clearing the old, thanking the Sun, honouring cattle, then visiting and community life.']},
{heading:'Why are sesame and jaggery shared?',paragraphs:['In Karnataka, Maharashtra and nearby regions, sesame and jaggery become festival foods. They suit the winter season, but the social gesture matters just as much. Something small, nourishing and sweet is exchanged between people.','Food is often where festival philosophy becomes practical. Gratitude is easier to remember when it can be cooked, shared and eaten.']},
{heading:'Why do people fly kites?',paragraphs:['Kite flying is especially famous in Gujarat. The sky becomes a public space, with terraces turning into gathering places. The practice is now one of the most recognisable regional expressions of Uttarayan.','It does not need a single ancient scriptural explanation to be meaningful. It is a living cultural tradition built around winter sunlight, community and play.']},
{heading:'What is the deeper meaning?',paragraphs:['Sankranti and Pongal are reminders that prosperity begins before the market. It begins with sunlight, rain, soil, seed, cattle, labour and the people who grow food.','A harvest festival makes dependence visible. That may be its most useful modern lesson.']}
],
