body:[
{paragraphs:['Vasant Panchami falls on 23 January in 2026. The name joins vasant, spring, with panchami, the fifth lunar day. In many homes, schools and temples, the day becomes Saraswati Puja.','Books, pens, musical instruments and tools of learning may be placed before the Goddess. Children may begin writing. Musicians offer their art. Yellow clothes and food bring spring into the room even when winter has not fully left.']},
{heading:'Why is Saraswati worshipped?',paragraphs:['Saraswati is associated with speech, knowledge, music and learning. That makes the festival less about passing an exam and more about the conditions that make understanding possible: attention, language, memory and disciplined practice.','For an artist, Saraswati is not simply inspiration. She also belongs to craft. A veena must still be practised. A book must still be opened.']},
{heading:'Why is yellow everywhere?',paragraphs:['Yellow is associated with the mustard fields of North India and with the colour of spring. Over time it became a visual shorthand for Vasant Panchami.','Clothing, sweets, flowers and decorations may all carry yellow. The custom is popular and regional rather than a universal scriptural command.']},
{heading:'What does the festival mean for children?',paragraphs:['Some families use Vasant Panchami for a child’s first formal writing or learning ritual. The details differ by language and region, but the idea is tender: beginning education should itself be sacred.','The ritual says that learning is more than competition. It is a relationship with knowledge.']},
{heading:'A simple way to observe it',paragraphs:['Keep a clean place for books or instruments. Offer flowers. Read or practise something with full attention. If Saraswati worship belongs to your family tradition, follow that.','The best offering to a goddess of learning is not decoration alone. It is sincere study.']}
],
