body:[
{paragraphs:['Maha Shivaratri falls on 15 February in 2026. The festival comes on the dark half of the lunar month and asks for something unusual: stay awake when the world normally sleeps.','Temples remain active late into the night. Shiva lingams are bathed. Bilva leaves are offered. Mantras repeat. Some people fast completely; others follow a lighter vrata. The outer practices differ, but the mood is one of sustained attention.']},
{heading:'What does Maha Shivaratri mean?',paragraphs:['Maha means great, Shiva names the deity, and ratri means night. The phrase is simply “the great night of Shiva.”','Its power comes partly from simplicity. The festival does not need daylight spectacle. Darkness, repetition and stillness are enough.']},
{heading:'Which story explains Shivaratri?',paragraphs:['There is no single universal story. Some traditions remember the marriage of Shiva and Parvati. Others connect the night with Shiva appearing as an endless column of light, the Jyotirlinga. Still others tell the story of a hunter whose accidental night-long worship becomes meritorious.','Rather than forcing these into one origin, it is more accurate to see Shivaratri as a festival that accumulated several Shiva traditions around one sacred night.']},
{heading:'Why fast and stay awake?',paragraphs:['Fasting reduces the day to essentials. Staying awake interrupts habit. Together they create a deliberate break in routine.','But austerity is not a competition. The point is attention. A person who cannot fast for health reasons can still pray, chant, listen or sit quietly.']},
{heading:'Why is the Shiva lingam worshipped?',paragraphs:['The lingam is one of Shiva’s central forms of worship. On Shivaratri it may receive repeated abhisheka with water, milk or other offerings according to temple and family tradition.','The form is not simply a decorative symbol. It carries a long ritual and philosophical history within Shaiva traditions.']},
{heading:'What can someone do at home?',paragraphs:['Light a lamp. Repeat Om Namah Shivaya. Read a Shiva stotra. Sit without the phone for a while. If you keep vigil, make the wakefulness meaningful rather than merely staying awake.','Shivaratri becomes powerful when the night is not filled with more noise.']}
],
